# Production settings for the dashboard on IIS (docs/DEPLOYMENT_IIS.md, section 7).
# The installer copies this to dashboard.env beside the backend's and the dashboard's folders when there is none, and
# limits who can read it. Node reads it when the site starts (--env-file). Fill in every blank. Never commit the copy,
# never paste it into a ticket or a chat, and save it as UTF-8.
# Until ALLOWED_EMAIL_DOMAINS names the client's own, nobody can sign in. Comments sit on their own lines: an inline
# comment after an empty value is read as the value, and a value must not contain # or quotes.
# The site's web.config sets OVERSIGHT_DATA=live, OVERSIGHT_API_URL and AUTH_MODE=atlassian itself, and they win over
# this file (sign-in is always on).

# The same value as API_SERVICE_KEY in the backend's .env.
OVERSIGHT_SERVICE_KEY=
# The client's Jira site, such as https://your-site.atlassian.net.
OVERSIGHT_JIRA_BASE_URL=
# The address people open: https, origin only, such as https://oversight.example.com. It is the installer's -HostName.
# Blank: /login names it as missing and nobody gets in.
APP_URL=
# The production Atlassian app's (section 5), never the development one's.
ATLASSIAN_CLIENT_ID=
ATLASSIAN_CLIENT_SECRET=
# At least 32 random characters (section 7), used nowhere else. Changing it signs everyone out and starts new viewer
# ids in the access log: export the log first.
AUTH_SECRET=
# The client's email domains only, comma-separated.
ALLOWED_EMAIL_DOMAINS=

# Who is an admin. Everyone who signs in is, unless you say otherwise. To make only some people admins (everyone else
# sees only their own Jira boards), uncomment both lines, with the client's admins on one of the domains above.
# ACCESS_MODE=admin-list
# ADMIN_EMAILS=

# Tests and the bundled sample only: leave every one of these empty.
ATLASSIAN_AUTH_URL=
ATLASSIAN_API_URL=
SAMPLE_NOW=
SAMPLE_LATENCY=
SAMPLE_PEOPLE_COMPARISON=
